  "modelingNotes": [
    "Queens Museum (the New York City Building, 1939) — original procedural geometry, CC0-1.0. bun generators/queens-museum.ts",
    "Authoring frame: x across the building (+x faces the Unisphere, east-north- east), y along its long axis, z up, metres. Catalog bearing 337.3°, anchor at the OSM outline's centroid. The outline (way/284856409, height 15.3) is a long body flanked by two wider end pavilions, each with a shallow curved flagpole niche in its long sides, and a lower block on each end.",
    "The building is mirrored end to end. Its two long faces differ: the east (park) face is a colonnade of square limestone piers between tall glazed bays, and the west (parkway) face carries the 2013 glass curtain. A broad skylight lantern over the central atrium sits on the roof.",
    "Original procedural model from https://github.com/alexwohlbruck/landmarks (catalog id \"queens-museum\", model \"queens-museum\"), generated by https://github.com/alexwohlbruck/landmarks/blob/c3921f733df14b41e1f6d15853cce7b343886f17/generators/queens-museum.ts. That repository is the editable source; source.blend.gz is the exported mesh imported into Blender for convenience.",
    "Exported for Open Landmarks v1: placement bearing and scale baked in; moving parts frozen in their rest pose; footings and basements below the lowest ground (y = 0) cut away; material colours carried over exactly, as library finishes where they are not standard swatches.",
    "To fit six materials, folded queens-museum-window into queens-museum-window-2 (ΔE 5)."
  ],
